From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Meta: Why I didn't file charges....

Just a brief note to let you folks know why I was not the one who filed any
charges against Carol Moore (or anyone else).

First, I am not in any way "protesting" the current systems of List
justice, although I've expressed my opinions to Harry about the possibility
that the justice system may become overly legalistic, with those who "color
outside the lines" getting zapped while True List Criminals (esp. Dale
Worley, but I'll get to this later) go unpunished. (Punishment is not
really the right word...it suggests a fixation on Foucault and his special
form of punishment: the Pit and the Pendulum.) Others have said much the
same thing. I favor treating the List as a large "private party," with the
Host (the ExI) or a "Welcoming Committee" having the authority--and
essentially the obligation--to kick people out who are seriously
disruptive. As with real parties, this is rarely necessary.

Second, Perry and Harry are consciously pushing the system in various ways
to try to improve the List. This is mostly their business.

Third, about me and Carol. Carol and I had a minor confrontation over my
"Klaus! von Future Prime" takeoffs on some ("sombunall" for you Wilson
fans) of her posts about aliens and reincarnation, and the Irish potato
famine post. I *thought* these were in relatively good humor (I didn't
scream "moron!" or "fool!" as we've seen in some recent flames), but I can
see why Carol would think I was taunting her.

In any case, as she angrily responded--I think she said I was being
"intellectually dishonest" at one point--I tried to brush it off. Such a
charge doesn't especially insult me. She sent me some fairly rude
e-mail--again, no List Crime--and I, perhaps foolishly, responded with my
own comments. After a bit, things settled down, and Carol and I have even
exchanged polite e-mail.

In my view, there was no chargeable offense. Disturbing the public order is
something Harry may have acted on, though. (Again, polycentric or market
law in action, as these issues of the "Crown" stepping in and making
charges are exactly those raised by Benson in "The Enterprise of Law."
There are arguable points as to whether it's good or bad for the Crown, or
Harry, or any other third party (like Perry, in this case), to step in when
the supposedly aggrieved party has no interest in pressing charges.)

Fourth, in my opinion, giving Carol 3 full warnings for whatever it is she
did seems a bit harsh. (But poor Harry must be really feeling dumped upon
with all these opinions. Like others have said, sorry, Harry, but we have
to say what we think. Questioning justice is a core principle here.)

Fifth, there's something wrong with a system that will allow Dale Worley to
waste our time, to toy with us, to ask sophistic points over and over
again, to admit that he will not make the time to read "Machinery of
Freedom" (even though he could read the whole thing in less time than he
was probably spending each day writing taunts to the List), and to openly
admit several times that he was basically baiting us!!

Many of us stopped responding to him (the famous "Boycott Dale Society,"
which mostly communicated with occasional private e-mail to other
prospective members--ironically, because posting such a call on the entire
list, and the reasons for it, perhaps would've been seen as flaming!), but
enough people did that he dominated the List for nearly two months. He
politely (Dale was always polite, to the point of being a crushing bore, in
my opinion) quit the last a while back and now is busy skewering us in
Pandit's various groups.

I speculated to Harry a few days ago that Dale's who net interaction had
the earmarks of someone doing an experiment on us, perhaps for a paper. If
not this, then he certainly was "toying" with us! (I may have posted
satires a few times, but you all know I'm not playing a manipulative head
game on you or the List!)

In my opinion, Dale Worley is an actual case of a List Criminal. I would
like to see a system that could allow us to get these obvious disrupters
off our list. Occasionally strange posts and occasional BBS-style rudeness
are *nothing* compared to Dale's behavior! (Pandit when he was just posting
about yogis and the Upanishads was fairly harmless. I was even beginning to
think some of us had been slightly too hard on him, as he was correct in
pointing out that he initially raised issue in connection with questions
about the intellectual education of Hans Moravec. His "escalation" to
actively seeking to undermine the ExI was a different matter.)

