Subject: Rational (racial) Discrimination

An incredibly effective AI, David Friedman writes: 

   To begin with, the issue is not "must" but probability.
	 If the odds that someone is about to commit a crime are, say, 40%, it is
	 reasonable to impose small costs on him (searching him to see if he has a
	 gun) in order to avoid large costs to the victim. Different legal
	 institutions might handle this in different ways, with or without
	 compensation to the innocent suspect, but no reasonable system will refuse
	 to take any action until the probability of guilt is 100%.

Oh right.  If the cops were accountable, then a reasonable balance
would likely be struck.  My normal short description of PPLs includes
this: "Buy insurance that the world will work the way you want it to,
and when it doesn't you get paid.  You premiums will depend on just
how unrealistic your world-view is, of course."

While this is probably not how PPLs would end up, it is an excellent
model for building intuitions.  In such a system, my friend would make
sure to get the package that included not getting harassed by cops.
Whenever they harrass him, he gets compensated.  His PPL then
negotiates with the cops PPL to come to adequate compensation.  This
would obviously be optimized away like most other things (to standard
compensation rates and fines on the cops if their PPL wasn't dominant,
etc.).  

Thank you.  The unfortunate conclusion is that in the current justice
system, no compensation will ever be made, and that particular
injustice will never be balanced.

