Date: Mon, 3 May 93 17:28:34 PDT
From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: TECH: Would `dark fiber' be vulnerable to jamming?

	 Beyond the ease of jamming, there's another problem.

	 Frequency allocation in radio only works because broadcasting equipment
	 is expensive, and broadcasters easy to track.  Even so, where enforcement
	 is poor, "pirate radio" can be a problem.

	 How much worse is it going to get on a "dumb fiber" network, where (if I've
	 got it right) *you can't reserve a frequency*?  *Anyone* could grab a
	 given channel just by broadcasting on it.

The answer to both problems is the optical equivalent of a diode.  You
only get broadcast info on the main channel.  Does that satisfy the
original goal?  If it does I suspect that the original goal wasn't
ambitious enough :-)

Instead of allocating bands, such a system should use non-sinusoidal
radio (similar to spread-spectrum, but better).

It seems like some equivalent of the optical diode could be much
simpler than an addressing network, but still let you divide up the
channel.  It doesn't let you adapt the topology of the channel so that
people who want more channel capacity can buy it.  I suspect the
hardware for routing networks will continue to go down in price so
that routing networks aren't a problem.

