From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: LIFE-X: the 120 yr limit (ex PUBS: SciAm, or something like that)

If antioxidants work in humans, they will likely increase the
maximum as well as the median lifespan.  The synthetic antioxidant 
propyl gallate increases the median lifespan of banana fruitflies 120%, 
and their maximum lifespan by 45%.  [Gerontology 1992;38:192-195].  Sodium 
selenite slightly increases their median and maximum lifespan, by 
increasing catalase and glutathione peroxidase levels (internally
manufactured antioxidant enzymes) [Gerontology 1989;35(4):188-191].   
Does anybody know of any research on the effects of propyl gallate
on mammals?

A buzzword now making the rounds of demographers is 
"the expansion of mortality": increase in variance of the average 
age at death.  In other words, those who beat the odds, thru 
effort or luck, are beating them by wider margins. Also, life expectancy
at age 85 is increasing, even as mean life expectancy is levelling off.
The "Gompertz curve", the assumption that death rates double 
every 8 years after age 30, may turn into a different curve, 
with slower increases in the death rate after age 85.   If the  
Gompertz curve holds up, even large decreases in age-specific mortality 
rates (85%) increase mean life expectancy by only 50 years.  It will be
interesting  to track the population of centenarians over the next
two decades, to see how strong this trend away from the Gompertz 
curve is, and where the increase in variance trend goes.  (The 
population  involved right now is still too small to get really 
good statistics from our notoriously  innacurate sampling methods 
like the Census). [Science v250, 1990] 

