Date: Tue, 4 May 1993 13:41:07 +0000
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk
Subject: Absolute morals (was Re: metaphysics vs epistemology)

The conundrum, "If there is no right and wrong, why shouldn't everyone do
anything they like?" is not new, and many philosophies which assert the
nonexistence of absolute, objective right and wrong have given broadly
similar answers.

First, I note that the question itself is ill-posed, presupposing by the
word "shouldn't" the existence of right and wrong which it denies. It also
differs only superficially from the question "If there isn't a State to
tell people what to do, why shouldn't everyone do anything they like?". I
take it that no-one here would be puzzled by the latter question for a
second. But that's by the way. 

Consider the following philosophies:

* Buddhism has the notion of karma, that certain actions will produce,
sooner or later, certain inevitable consequences which, if the actor but
saw them clearly, would persuade him to adopt certain principles of conduct
(nonviolence, compassion, mindfulness, and so on). Accordingly, Buddhists
speak not of right and wrong, but of skilful or unskilful actions and
states of mind.

* Crowleyans say "Do what thou wilt is the whole of the Law", but have the
notion of one's True Will, distinct from one's deluded false will, and
which one must struggle to perceive. (Owen Rowley might speak further on
this if he were still here.)

* Voltaire spoke of "enlightened self-interest".

* Some libertarians claim that certain principles of conduct will
spontaneously emerge, if they are only allowed to do so, such as property
rights and being nice to strangers.

* Finally, I note an interesting episode in E.E. "Doc" Smith's Lensman
series of space-opera SF. I don't remember which book, but this is the
situation: After a climactic battle, the forces of good defeat the forces
of evil. However, while the small fry on the evil side can be
rehabilitated, their leaders pose a problem, because some of them have been
captured alive. What to do with them? Executing them or locking them up
permanently would be barbaric, and a waste of their undeniable talents. So
Kimball Kinnison (the leader of the good guys) simply shows the evil leader
(whose name I forget) the good guys' civilisation: how the people live, how
the government works, everything. After this grand tour, he asks the evil
leader whose civilisation he prefers. The evil leader finally admits that
the good guys have it better. End of problem. No shoulds, no retribution,
no moral arguments, no philosophy; none necessary.

