From: eisrael@suneast.East.Sun.COM (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: DAISY: the death of Hal 9000

Michal Clive Price mentions an article in SciAm concerning the "death"
of computer neural nets:
>      As the network approached death [>90% of connections severed], it
>      began to output not gibberish but information it had previously
>      learned - its silicon life flashed before it eyes, so to speak.

I found this little snippet fascinating (I read the original in SciAm
when it appeared) and it seems clear to me that this is very suggestive
evidence for the materialist conception of consciousness. In fact, I
think that this line of thinking probably explains many of the
so-called "near death experiences" that people have sometimes used to
"prove" a more dualist outlook.

Things like floating, seeing bright lights, having your life flash
before your eyes, etc., are explainable psychological phenomena that
need not lead one to the conclusion that there is life after death.
Which makes me all the more certain that while I do look forward to
augmenting my central nervous system, I don't want to replace it.

