It seems to me that we have well started -- certainly I'm about to
wind up and take a shot at it ...

[...]
|> I'd rather have adjudication done by a group, perhaps of three
|> people.  None of us are judges; it would be nice to have some brake
|> to help us keep our whims out of our decisions.

In a real PPL, arbitration can be done by any member (or panel)
willing to serve and agreeable to the disputants, arbitrators are
compensated, and part of their income then depends on their reputation
for incorruptibility, knowledge of the law, and _blindness to
irrelevant factors_ such as race, creed, wealth, reputation, etc., to
the extent that they are irrelevant.

As a result, highly competent professional arbitors rise to the top, a
spontaneous order which enhances the larger order. Now, if we can just
stop them from forming a guild and trying to make rules prohibiting
non-members from the practice ... (can you say "Bar Association"?)

Forcing all complaints to be handled by the "State" invalidates our
experiment from the beginning, as Harry has divided loyalty in his
arbitration practice -- he is trying to settle the matter at hand and
enforce the list code for its own sake, in his capacity as ExI Board
representative. A true arbitrator does not represent any third party,
whether it be a person, corporation, or government.

Now, if what the board _wants_ is someone to monitor the list and hand
out warnings for _all_ infractions, then let's do _that_, and drop the
pretense of PPL. Oh, I've just reinvented a moderated list, haven't I?
If I remember correctly, no one wanted that the last time it came up,
and I doubt that anyone wants the moderator's job, either.
