Date: Tue, 4 May 1993 09:32:50 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Meta: Judgment Metzger vs. Moore

>In a real PPL, arbitration can be done by any member (or panel)
>willing to serve and agreeable to the disputants, . . .

This is somewhat misleading, as it implies that disputants can always
choose their arbitrators afresh.  But a PPL may be set up such that the
arbitrator or private judge is chosen long before the dispute arises,
without any option to opt for another judge.  Members of a PPL may find
this precommitment strategy offers special benefits, and thus freely agree
to it.  That's all we need for a PPL, the hallmark of which is that it
arises through explicitly consensual arrangements.

This list's legal system thus qualifies as a PPL, as disputants have agreed
to have Harry hear their dispute by freely joining the list.

I'm pleased to see that so many people are giving careful thought to how we
can improve this list's legal processes.  There have been some great
suggestions, and I'm sure Harry will take them seriously.  I have to agree
with Tim Starr, though, that folks ought to appeal to the list adjudicator
before they assume that Harry's decisions are writ in stone.

