Subject: LIFE-X: the 120 yr limit (ex PUBS: SciAm, or something like that)

Does anyone know why it is the Gompertz Law which is supposed to govern 
human mortality? It essentially says that the force of morality (negative
logarithmic derivative of the cohort size as a function of age) is entirely
determined by its value at age zero (or some other given age) and the
amount of time it takes for it to double. In other words, force of mortality
is assumed to be an exponential function of age. The point is that this is
not true if you take all data (males have a local maximum of force around
the ages 17-25, because they drive, but you can take accidental deaths out,
and then again taking them out may distort the picture, as by doing so
you do not give them a chance to die of natural causes, there is also
a drop in force of mortality after age 85, etc.). Why Gompertz then?
The only reason I see is computational, running regressions on data is easier
if you assume that form.

